  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n===== Cold Front Passage Today =====\n\nAn upper level trough is situated over the central US this morning, \nand will be the driving force for a sfc low over the Great Lakes to \neventually bring a cold front through our area later this morning. \nEarly on, cloud cover is on the increase ahead of this boundary, \nwhich is expected to bring low stratus over the area by sunrise and \nthrough the morning hours. Temps have been holding steady this \nmorning so far, with readings hovering in the mid-70s since last \nevening. However, SPC mesoanalysis shows the sfc boundary has passed \nthrough the St. Louis area, with KBLV showing a nearly 20F \ntemperature drop in just 3 hours, showcasing the strong CAA \nfollowing just behind this front this morning. \n\nToday will feature some interesting weather as the front passes \nthrough later on this morning. First, expect temps to drop throughout \nthe day as CAA regime takes over with FROPA. Our highest temps of \nthe day likely are occurring early this morning within the warmer pre-\nfrontal airmass. Second, guidance supports this FROPA to feature an \nanafront, with the majority of precip occurring after the boundary \npushes through. Mid-level vorticity max lags behind with the trough \naxis, displacing the better forcing aloft later this morning. \nAdditionally, low level moisture will outrun the better forcing, \nleading to mainly light rain showers this morning, and lingering \neats of I-65 this afternoon. Total QPF is expected to be less than \n0.10\" for most today. Our probabilistic precip forecast has a \ngeneral 30% chance of exceeding 0.1\" today, and less than a 15% of \nexceeding 0.25\". Third, winds will become quite breezy today due to \nthe compact sfc pressure gradient with the frontal passage. NW wind \ngusts up to 25-30 mph will be possible, and will also boost the CAA \ninfiltration. \n\n\n===== Cooler Fall Air Finally Arrives This Weekend =====\n\nWith the help of the FROPA today, we'll finally break the hold that \nthe above normal temperatures had on our region and start feeling \nsome seasonable temperatures by the weekend. Upper ridging and a \nlarge dome of high pressure will expand across the central US, and \nwith our cooler post-frontal airmass in place, much cooler temps are \nexpected over the weekend and into next week. High pressure will \nkeep us dry over the next several days as well. \n\nExpect highs on Saturday in the 50s, though a touch warmer on Sunday \nwith temps peaking into the 60s. The main feature to keep tabs on \nover the weekend will be the high potential for frost Sunday morning \nwith morning lows forecast down into the 30s. No Frost Advisory yet \nwith this forecast package, but confidence seems high enough that \nwe'll eventually need one sometime before Saturday night. \n\nTemperatures will gradually climb back into the low 70s by late next \nweek. There are no precip chances through next week as sfc high \npressure keeps a strong hold over a large portion of the country.",
